// A FIELD DEFAULT NAMING A BARE NULLARY VARIANT — ACCEPTED, and the
// default actually applied.
//
// `Blue` names no function and reads no frame: it is a value of a
// DECLARATION, exactly what `is_pure_access` admits the qualified
// `Shade.Blue` for. The gate refused it anyway, because a bare name
// arrives as a `Name` node and `is_pure_value` had no arm for one — so
// every struct whose default was spelled bare was unconstructible,
// diagnostic pointing here rather than at the construction site.
// std.build.Exe (`out :: Res<Path> = None`) is that bug wearing a
// module's name.
//
// The gate must answer for precisely the names the lowering can write:
// frame locals are refused (the use site is not in their frame), and
// anything else goes only when the same walk lower_global_value runs —
// defs_of first, then variant_defs — answers with a constant or an
// enum, and `None` stays special because `Res` is structural and its
// constructors resolve through NO declaration walk to refuse them.
//
// The defaults are NON-ZERO and each field is read back, so a dropped
// default cannot pass as C zeroing; `Shade.Green` rides along as the
// qualified spelling of the same rule, already legal before this fix.

Error = AllocError | IoError

Shade = Red | Green | Blue

Box = {
    n*: i32,
    hue*:: Shade = Blue,
}

main = (env: Env) Res<i32, Error> {
    b = Box(n: 5);
    b.hue.match({
        Red   => println("red"),
        Green => println("green"),
        Blue  => println("blue"),
    });

    // the qualified spelling of the same value, supplied over the bare
    // default: both forms are one rule, not two.
    g = Box(n: 6, hue: Shade.Green);
    g.hue.match({
        Red   => println("red"),
        Green => println("green"),
        Blue  => println("blue"),
    });

    Ok(0);
}
